============================ Pipeline Creating


. Discard old builds: 
This determines when, if ever, build records for this project should be discarded. Build records include the console output, archived artifacts,
and any other metadata related to a particular build. Keeping fewer builds means less disk space will be used in the Build Record Root Directory ,
which is specified on the Configure System screen.

   Jenkins offers two options for determining when builds should be discarded:

   - Build age: discard builds when they reach a certain age; for example, seven days old.
   - Build count: discard the oldest build when a certain number of builds already exist.

   Log rotation Strategy: discard old build by log ratation strategy  if jenkins taking much space.
 

. Do not allow concurrent builds:
  "Means one controller that check which job should run or not"
  "disableResume()" — a Jenkins pipeline option that stops the build from resuming after a controller restart, instead marking it as failed/aborted.

 A Jenkinsfile/pipeline option (options { disableResume() }) that prevents a running pipeline from resuming after the Jenkins controller restarts, 
 forcing it to fail or be re-triggered instead of picking up where it left off.
 

. Pipeline speed/durability override: 
  It's a setting that lets you choose between a faster pipeline or a safer pipeline — safer means it can survive a Jenkins restart, 
  faster means it skips that safety to run quicker.
                                                    or
 This setting allows users to change the default durability mode for running Pipelines. 
 In most cases this is a trade-off between performance and the ability for running pipelines to resume after unplanned Jenkins outages.

. Preserve stashes from completed builds: 
  A setting that keeps temporary files (stashes) from old, finished builds instead of deleting them right away 
  — normally Jenkins clears them out to save space, but this option holds onto them longer.

. Throttle builds:
  A setting that limits how many builds can run at the same time (or how fast they run), so the system doesn't get overloaded.

. Triggers:
Settings that tell Jenkins when to automatically start a build — like on a schedule, when code changes, 
 or after another job finishes — instead of someone starting it manually.
    - Build after other projects are built – Runs this job automatically after a specified job finishes.
    - Build periodically – Runs this job on a fixed schedule (like cron), regardless of code changes.
    - GitHub hook trigger for GITScm polling – Runs this job instantly when GitHub sends a push notification (webhook).
    - Poll SCM – Checks the repository at set intervals and runs the job only if new changes are found.
    - Trigger builds remotely (e.g., from scripts) – Lets an external script or tool start this job using a special URL/token.


